  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe front that brought rainfall yesterday continues to move \neast of the region. High clouds remain over potions of north- \ncentral Indiana, but will gradually move east through the \nevening. There is a narrow band of convergence that extends from\nsouthern Lake Michigan down to Gibson City, Illinois resulting \nin mid-level cumulus clouds. Clearer skies extend northwest of \nthe area as today marks the first of several with generally dry\nconditions. \n\nWinds remain out of the northeast around 10 mph, and are\nexpected to diminish after sunset. With clear skies, light \nwinds, and cooling temperatures, there is a chance for patchy \nshallow fog away from the Chicago metropolitan area. Widespread\ndense fog is not expected, but satellite trends will need to be\nmonitored overnight. Otherwise, the northerly flow will advect \nin a cooler air mass knocking temperatures down into the 40s to \nlocally upper 30s tonight. \n\nSeasonable temperatures look to set-up through next week with \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s and overnight \nlows in the upper 40s and lower 50s. Monday night does look\ncooler with potential for overnight lows locally in the upper\n30s. Ensemble models are showing some consistency with a \ncouple of fronts passing through the western Great Lakes: one on\nSunday, the other in the middle of the week. It is possible\neither provide an isolated shower or sprinkle, though chances\nlook comparatively better in Wisconsin. \n\nThe dry pattern is favored to continue into the second full week\nof October with chances for rain returning sometime in the \nOctober 11-13 timeframe.\n\nDK",
